# Persistence Adapters

This Livebook stores an Agent checkpoint, loads it, and logically deletes it.
It uses the ETS adapter, which is suitable for local development and tests.

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Define a portable Agent

```elixir
defmodule PersistenceGuide.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "persistence_counter"

  agent do
    schema Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    route "increment" do
      action %{by: by},
        name: "persistence_guide_increment",
        schema: Zoi.object(%{by: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(1)}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: context.agent_state.count + by}}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

Create and change the Agent as a value. No actor is necessary for this step.

```elixir
agent = PersistenceGuide.Counter.new!(id: "guide-counter")
signal = Jido.Signal.new!("increment", %{by: 4}, source: "/guides/persistence")
{:ok, agent, []} = PersistenceGuide.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)
agent.state
```

## Save through an adapter

An adapter configuration is a module or `{module, options}` pair. The options
below select a table only for this guide.

```elixir
store = {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :persistence_guide}

:ok =
  Jido.Persistence.save_agent(
    store,
    agent,
    revision: 1,
    instance: PersistenceGuide.Jido
  )
```

Jido owns the key format, active and tombstone record meaning, validation,
encoding, and revision check. An adapter only stores binary keys and binary
values. Runtime adapters must implement binary `get/2` and atomic
`compare_and_swap/4`. A `get/2` result can also return an opaque binary token
with the bytes. Jido checks the record and then passes that token to the
adapter's conditional write. Adapters that return bytes only keep exact-byte
comparison. `put/3` and `delete/2` are optional maintenance operations.

## Load and logically delete

The same instance and partition values identify the record on every operation.

```elixir
{:ok, restored} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent(
    store,
    PersistenceGuide.Counter,
    "guide-counter",
    instance: PersistenceGuide.Jido
  )

restored.state
```

```elixir
:ok =
  Jido.Persistence.delete_agent(
    store,
    PersistenceGuide.Counter,
    "guide-counter",
    instance: PersistenceGuide.Jido
  )

{:error, :deleted} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent(
    store,
    PersistenceGuide.Counter,
    "guide-counter",
    instance: PersistenceGuide.Jido
  )
```

Normal delete replaces the exact active record with a compact tombstone. The
tombstone contains identity and revision, but no checkpoint or Agent state. It
prevents a delayed normal writer from recreating that record lifetime. Delete
of a missing identity creates a revision-zero tombstone.

Physical tombstone purge is a storage maintenance task. Core does not use it
for normal Agent deletion.

## Choose an adapter

Jido includes these adapters:

| Adapter | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `Jido.Persistence.ETS` | Tests and local development. Data ends with the BEAM. |
| `Jido.Persistence.File` | One local BEAM with no shared writers. |
| `Jido.Persistence.Redis` | Shared durable storage through an application-supplied command function. |
| `Jido.Persistence.S3` | Direct single-object checkpoints for infrequent writes through an application-supplied signed request function. |
| `Jido.Persistence.Bedrock` | Shared durable storage through Bedrock KV 0.7 transactions. |
| `Jido.Persistence.Ecto` | PostgreSQL or SQLite through an application Ecto repository. |

Configure persistence on a Jido instance when most actors use the same store.
An Agent start option can override or disable that configuration.

```elixir
defmodule PersistenceGuide.AppJido do
  use Jido,
    otp_app: :my_app,
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :my_app_agents}
end
```

The ETS adapter creates its table when first used. The File adapter is for one
local writer domain. The Redis adapter lets the host application own its Redis
client and connection policy. A Redis TTL applies to tombstones too. Thus, it
also limits the duration of the delayed-writer fence.

## Configure direct S3 checkpoints

Give the S3 adapter a bucket and a function that signs and sends one request:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
persistence =
  {Jido.Persistence.S3,
   bucket: "my-agent-checkpoints",
   prefix: "production/agents/",
   timeout_ms: 5_000,
   request_fn: &MyApp.S3Client.one_request/1}
```

The request function receives one map with `method`, `bucket`, `key`,
`headers`, `body`, `timeout_ms`, `retry: false`, and
`follow_redirects: false`. It returns `{:ok, %{status: status, headers:
headers, body: body, code: code}}` or an error. `code` is the parsed S3 error
code for failed requests. See `Jido.Persistence.S3` for exact response rules.
The host owns the endpoint, credentials, signing, HTTP client, and connection
pool. It must stop requests by the given timeout and must not retry a
conditional write. A lost write reply is indeterminate, even if a later read
finds the same bytes. Keep credentials, object bodies, ETags, and signed
headers out of client logs and telemetry.

The adapter uses `If-None-Match: *` to create an object and `If-Match` with
the ETag from the validated read to replace it. It sends a SHA-256 checksum
on writes and checks the returned checksum on reads. The endpoint must
support these conditions and return the checksum. Test each S3 provider and
configuration before use; a MinIO pass alone does not prove AWS S3 behavior.
Keep active objects and tombstones from automatic expiry. S3 versioning can
retain old versions and make maintenance deletion create a delete marker.
Normal Agent deletion remains a conditional tombstone write. This adapter
uses one object per key and limits each object to 5,000,000 bytes. It suits
checkpoints with infrequent writes, not a low-latency write path.

## Configure the Ecto adapter

Ecto SQL is an optional Jido dependency. Add Ecto SQL and the driver for the
application database. For PostgreSQL, add these dependencies:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
def deps do
  [
    {:jido, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ecto_sql, "~> 3.14"},
    {:postgrex, "~> 1.0"}
  ]
end
```

The application owns and supervises its Ecto repository. Jido does not start
the repository and does not run migrations. Create this table before a Jido
instance uses the adapter:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Repo.Migrations.CreateJidoPersistenceRecords do
  use Ecto.Migration

  def change do
    create table(:jido_persistence_records, primary_key: false) do
      add :key, :binary, primary_key: true, null: false
      add :value, :binary, null: false
      add :write_token, :binary, null: false
    end
  end
end
```

The `key` and `value` columns keep the exact adapter bytes. The primary key
makes create-only writes atomic. `write_token` is private adapter data. It lets
a same-value CAS report one changed row. Do not add timestamps or other stored
fields to the default schema.

The repository database role must have direct select, insert, update, and
delete access to this table. Do not add a trigger or rule that hides rows,
suppresses writes, or changes the three adapter columns. CAS uses the database
affected-row result.

Configure the repository on the Jido instance:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Jido do
  use Jido,
    otp_app: :my_app,
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.Ecto, repo: MyApp.Repo}
end
```

Use `repo_options: [prefix: "tenant"]` when the table uses an Ecto query
prefix. Use `schema: MyApp.JidoPersistenceRecord` for a different table name.
The custom schema must have only the binary `key`, `value`, and `write_token`
fields, and `key` must be its single primary key.

The Ecto adapter supports the PostgreSQL and SQLite Ecto adapters. It rejects
other database adapters during configuration validation because their upsert
and affected-row rules do not prove this CAS contract.

## Configure Bedrock KV

Bedrock support is optional. Its core adapter unit tests remain active. Real
Bedrock service checks, including previously passing single-node storage and
transaction cases, are paused in this beta pending upstream fixes. Strict
multi-node startup and MinIO-backed snapshot recovery are not verified. Known
upstream defects are
tracked in [Bedrock issue 319](https://github.com/bedrock-kv/bedrock/issues/319)
and [Jido issue 370](https://github.com/agentjido/jido/issues/370). Do not rely
on this beta for those durability paths until fixed and retested.

Add the supported `0.7.x` client to the host
application:

```elixir
def deps do
  [
    {:jido, "~> 3.0"},
    {:bedrock, "~> 0.7.2"},
    {:bedrock_raft, "~> 0.10.0", override: true}
  ]
end
```

The explicit Bedrock Raft dependency keeps the host on the tested `0.10.x`
client API used by Bedrock `0.7.2` and later `0.7.x` releases.

Define the Bedrock cluster and repo. The repo must use `Bedrock.Repo`:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.BedrockCluster do
  use Bedrock.Cluster, otp_app: :my_app, name: "my_app"
end

defmodule MyApp.BedrockRepo do
  use Bedrock.Repo, cluster: MyApp.BedrockCluster
end
```

Configure the Bedrock cluster for the deployment. Bedrock uses strict
durability mode by default. Keep strict mode for production. It validates log
replication and persistent coordinator, log, and materializer paths when the
cluster starts. Follow the Bedrock durability guide for local file or
S3-compatible object storage settings.

Add the cluster before the Jido instance in the application supervision tree:

```elixir
children = [MyApp.BedrockCluster, MyApp.Jido]
```

Select the repo in the Jido instance:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Jido do
  use Jido,
    otp_app: :my_app,
    persistence:
      {Jido.Persistence.Bedrock,
       repo: MyApp.BedrockRepo,
       prefix: "my-app:jido:",
       timeout_in_ms: 5_000}
end
```

The prefix defaults to `"jido:persistence:"`. It must be a non-empty binary
in the Bedrock user keyspace. The timeout must be a positive integer in
milliseconds or `:infinity`. The adapter rejects a stored key larger than
16 KiB and a value larger than 128 KiB before it starts a transaction.

The adapter uses one Bedrock transaction for each CAS. `Repo.get/1` adds the
read conflict, and `Repo.put/2` writes the new exact bytes. Bedrock normally
retries transaction failures. The adapter sets `retry_limit: 0` for writes
because a lost commit reply can have an unknown result. It reports a resolver
abort as a confirmed conflict. It reports every other write failure as
indeterminate. Jido then stops the affected activation and requires restore
before another write.

Each adapter operation runs in a fresh process. It cannot join an application
Bedrock transaction and report success before that outer transaction commits.
The adapter stores the Jido bytes without an envelope, so `get/2` returns the
exact bytes used by CAS.

A successful Bedrock transaction is strictly serializable. Bedrock 0.7 reports
success after every required log has appended and synced its write-ahead log.
The cluster durability profile still controls deployment durability. The
adapter does not make Bedrock relaxed mode durable.

